After the restoration, I have not been able in all this time to find a reliable way of detecting my phone in the open or closed position and having the applicaton resize accordingly. It has never worked right.
I had an AI do deep resarch on several pages I found that discussed the topic and had it write for me a report on what it was able to glean to accomplish this task once and for all.

Below is that report:

Programmatic Detection of Foldable Device States in Basic4Android (B4A) via Jetpack WindowManager1. The Paradigm Shift in Mobile Display ArchitectureThe mobile computing landscape has undergone a radical transformation with the introduction and commercial maturation of foldable devices. Led by Samsung’s Galaxy Z Fold and Z Flip series, this hardware category challenges the foundational assumptions of mobile application development. Historically, the "screen" was an immutable physical constant—a rigid rectangle with fixed dimensions, pixel density, and aspect ratio. Developers could safely assume that DisplayMetrics retrieved at application launch would remain valid throughout the session, or at most, swap between two fixed states (Portrait and Landscape) upon rotation.The foldable device dismantles this static model. The display is now a dynamic topological surface capable of altering its physical geometry, logical coordinate space, and user interaction model in real-time. This shift necessitates a move from "responsive" design—which largely meant adapting to different static screen sizes—to "adaptive" continuity, where an application must flow seamlessly between physical states without losing context or user intent.1.1. The Biology of the Fold: Hardware to SoftwareTo understand the software challenge, one must first appreciate the hardware reality. A device like the Samsung Galaxy Z Fold 5 is not simply a phone that gets wider. It is a device that transitions between distinct "postures." The hinge mechanism is equipped with highly precise Hall effect sensors and gyroscopes that measure the angle between the two display halves.However, the raw data from these sensors is noisy. A user holding the device might naturally fluctuate the angle between 175° and 180°. If the software were to react to raw sensor data, the UI would flicker uncontrollably between "Flat" and "Folded" states. Furthermore, the physical hinge is not the only variable; the "fold" in software terms is defined by the occlusion of content or the separation of logical touch areas. On the Microsoft Surface Duo, the fold is a physical gap (occlusion) of 84 pixels. On the Samsung Z Fold, the fold is a continuous surface with a visible crease but no pixel loss.This hardware fragmentation created an urgent need for a unified software abstraction. In the early days (circa 2019), OEMs exposed custom, proprietary APIs. Samsung had its own SDK, Microsoft had another, and Huawei a third. This "API hell" made cross-device development nearly impossible. Google’s response was the Jetpack WindowManager library (androidx.window), a unified API surface that abstracts the underlying hardware differences into a standardized set of semantic states.1.2. The Significance of Posture DetectionFor a Basic4Android (B4A) developer, detecting these states is not merely about resizing a layout. It is about unlocking the unique utility of the form factor.Tabletop Mode (Half-Open Horizontal): A device sitting on a desk at 90° acts like a mini-laptop. The app should react by placing content (video, text) on the top half and controls (keyboard, playback buttons) on the bottom half.Book Mode (Half-Open Vertical): A device held like a paperback. A reading app should split text into two columns, mimicking pages, rather than stretching one line across the crease.Flex Mode (Samsung Specific): This is Samsung’s marketing term for the system-level behaviors triggered by the HALF_OPENED state. If an app does not strictly handle this, OneUI (Samsung's Android skin) may force its own generic controls, disrupting the app's native experience.The urgency of the user's request stems from this reality: standard detection methods fail to distinguish these nuances. A "width change" event could be a fold, but it could also be a split-screen activation, a floating window resize, or a rotation. Only by querying the specific posture can the application react correctly.2. The Failure of Web-Based Heuristics in Native WrappersThe user's original query highlights a critical pain point: "We are having a hard time detecting fold/unfold events in a B4A application with inline JavaScript." This is a common but ultimately doomed approach in the hybrid app ecosystem. To understand why the native solution is non-negotiable, we must deeply analyze the failure modes of the web-based approach.2.1. The WebView Viewport IllusionIn a hybrid B4A app, the UI is often rendered within a WebView component. Developers accustomed to web technologies naturally reach for JavaScript solutions like window.matchMedia or window.innerWidth to detect changes.The fundamental problem lies in the decoupling of the Layout Viewport from the Visual Viewport.The Layout Viewport: This is the canvas the browser draws onto. Its size is determined by the <meta name="viewport"> tag.The Visual Viewport: This is what the user actually sees.When a Samsung Z Fold transitions from folded (cover screen) to unfolded (main screen), the physical screen width effectively doubles. However, the WebView component inside the Android View hierarchy does not strictly "resize" in the way a desktop browser window does. The Android Activity configuration change destroys and recreates the Activity (by default), or, if configChanges is intercepted, the View hierarchy goes through a complex invalidation pass.During this transition, there is a race condition. The JavaScript engine inside the WebView may fire a resize event based on the intermediate state of the animation, or it may retain the cached viewport width of the cover screen until the layout pass is fully settled. Consequently, inline JavaScript often reports the old width (320px or 360px) milliseconds after the device has already unfolded to 600px+.2.2. The Inadequacy of CSS Media QueriesRecent proposals for CSS Media Queries Level 5 include "Screen Spanning" features (@media (spanning: single-fold-vertical)). However, these standards are still in draft status and implementation varies wildly across WebView versions (Chrome, System WebView, Samsung Internet).Samsung's WebView Implementation: Samsung devices use a specific implementation of the System WebView. While standard Chrome might support experimental foldable APIs, the embedded WebView used by B4A apps often lags behind or requires specific manifest flags (android:resizeableActivity="true") to even acknowledge that the screen size can change.Pixel Density Confusion: Foldable screens often have different pixel densities on the cover screen versus the main screen. JavaScript's window.devicePixelRatio changes during the fold. If the B4A app does not handle the density change natively, the WebView might scale the content incorrectly, reporting a logical width that suggests a phone even when on the tablet-sized inner screen.2.3. Why "Inline JavaScript" Cannot Detect PostureEven if the resize event were perfectly reliable, it suffers from a semantic deficit. A resize event tells the code: "The screen is now 800px wide." It does not tell the code: "The screen is bent at 90 degrees."A Samsung Z Fold 5 in "Tabletop Mode" (bent 90°) has the exact same viewport resolution as a Z Fold 5 that is fully flat but displaying a split-screen app on the top half.JavaScript: Sees width=800, height=400.Reality:Scenario A: Flat screen, split window. (Safe to draw across the middle).Scenario B: Bent screen, Tabletop. (Unsafe to draw across the middle; it's the crease).JavaScript in a standard WebView has no access to the Hardware Sensor Manager or the WindowManager's FoldingFeature data. Therefore, it is physically impossible for inline JavaScript to distinguish between "Small Window" and "Bent Screen." This necessitates the native B4A/Java solution.3. Android's Native Response: The Jetpack WindowManager LibraryThe solution to the limitations of sensors and web views is the Jetpack WindowManager. This library is the canonical source of truth for modern Android form factors.3.1. Architecture of androidx.windowThe library operates on a client-server model.The Server (Extensions): The "Sidecar" or "Extensions" module (androidx.window.extensions) is a vendor-specific library pre-installed on the device by the OEM (Samsung). It has privileged access to the hardware drivers and hinge sensors.The Client (Jetpack Library): The app includes the androidx.window library. This library connects to the OEM Extensions, normalizes the data, and exposes it to the developer.This separation is crucial. It means that code written using Jetpack WindowManager on a Samsung device will work seamlessly on a Google Pixel Fold or a Motorola Razr, because the library handles the translation of proprietary sensor data into standard "Features."3.2. Data Structures: WindowLayoutInfo & FoldingFeatureThe core unit of information is the WindowLayoutInfo object. Unlike legacy metrics which described the whole screen, this object describes the window's relationship to the display features.The WindowLayoutInfo contains a list of DisplayFeature objects. The most relevant implementation is FoldingFeature.
Key Attributes of FoldingFeature :Bounds (Rect): The coordinate rectangle of the fold. On a Z Fold, the height might be full-screen, but the width might be 0 (a hairline crease). On a Surface Duo, the width is significant (the bezel gap).State (FoldingFeature.State):FLAT: The feature is present but the device is fully open. (Note: On some devices, a generic "flat" state might result in an empty list of features, implying continuity).HALF_OPENED: The critical state for "Flex Mode." The hinge is angled.Orientation (FoldingFeature.Orientation):HORIZONTAL: The fold runs left-to-right (Clamshell/Laptop style).VERTICAL: The fold runs top-to-bottom (Book style).OcclusionType:NONE: The screen is continuous (Samsung).FULL: The screen is physically separated (Surface Duo).isSeparating: A derived boolean. If TRUE, the UI should logically split. This is true for HALF_OPENED states or OcclusionType.FULL.3.3. The Observable PatternUnlike DisplayMetrics which are pulled synchronously, WindowLayoutInfo is pushed asynchronously. The state of the fold can change at any millisecond. Therefore, the API does not offer a getFoldState() method. Instead, it offers a registerListener or Flow pattern. The application subscribes to updates, and the WindowManager fires a callback whenever the physical geometry changes.4. The Interoperability Crisis: Kotlin vs. Legacy Java EnvironmentsThe user's requirement is to implement this in Basic4Android (B4A). This introduces a significant interoperability barrier that is not present in standard Android Studio development.4.1. The Kotlin-First StrategySince 2019, Google has adopted a "Kotlin First" strategy for Jetpack libraries. The androidx.window library is written in Kotlin. Its primary API surface utilizes Kotlin Coroutines and Flows.The Problem: B4A's "Inline Java" functionality utilizes a standard Java compiler (javac). It does not have access to the Kotlin runtime or Coroutine scopes. A B4A developer cannot write lifecycleScope.launch {... } inside an #If JAVA block.The Symptom: Attempting to use the standard WindowInfoTracker.getOrCreate(context).windowLayoutInfo(activity) method from Java results in a compilation error because the return type is a Flow, a type that is effectively alien to a standard Java 7/8 environment without massive dependency overhead.4.2. B4A's Architecture and "Desugaring"B4A wraps the Android SDK but maintains a simplified, procedural language structure. When "Inline Java" is used, the B4A compiler inserts that code into the generated Java source files before compilation.However, the androidx.window library (and its dependencies) utilizes Java 8 bytecode features (Default Interface Methods, Lambdas). Older Android devices (pre-API 24) and older compiler toolchains do not support these features natively. This process is called "Desugaring"—rewriting modern bytecode to be compatible with older VMs.If a B4A user creates a wrapper for androidx.window but fails to enable Desugaring or fails to include the correct core libraries, the app will crash at runtime with AbstractMethodError or NoClassDefFoundError when the WindowManager tries to invoke a callback. This is a frequent stumbling block for B4A developers trying to "copy-paste" Java code from StackOverflow.4.3. The Solution: androidx.window:window-javaTo support the vast ecosystem of Java-based apps (and game engines like Unity), Google released a specific artifact: androidx.window:window-java.This library is the "Rosetta Stone" for B4A developers. It provides a class named WindowInfoTrackerCallbackAdapter.Function: It wraps the Kotlin Flow.Exposure: It exposes addWindowLayoutInfoListener which accepts a standard Java Consumer<WindowLayoutInfo> and an Executor.Impact: This allows B4A developers to write pure, standard Java 7/8 code to interact with the modern foldable API, completely bypassing the need for Kotlin Coroutines.5. Architectural Solution: The Callback Adapter PatternTo satisfy the user's request for a "B4A coding agent prompt," we must first architect the exact Java solution that the agent will be instructed to generate. This solution uses the Callback Adapter Pattern.5.1. The ComponentsThe solution requires three distinct components interacting within the B4A Inline Java block:The Lifecycle Manager: A class (e.g., FoldableListener) that ties the listener to the Activity's lifecycle (onStart / onStop). This is critical; failing to unregister the listener leads to memory leaks and crashes when the Activity is destroyed on rotation (if not handled).The Main Thread Executor: The WindowManager requires an Executor to determine where to run the callback. Since B4A is single-threaded (UI thread driven), we must provide an executor that posts runnables to the Main Looper.The Consumer: The callback itself, which receives the data, parses the FoldingFeature, and dispatches the event back to the B4A layer.5.2. Detailed Analysis of the MainThreadExecutorIn standard Java concurrent programming, one might use Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor(). However, using a background thread for this task is dangerous in B4A. If the callback fires on a background thread, and the developer tries to update a B4A Label or Panel immediately, the app will crash with CalledFromWrongThreadException.The safe implementation, validated by snippets  and , is to define a custom executor that wraps an Android Handler:Javapublic class MainThreadExecutor implements java.util.concurrent.Executor {
   private final android.os.Handler handler = new android.os.Handler(android.os.Looper.getMainLooper());
   @Override
   public void execute(Runnable command) {
      handler.post(command);
   }
}
This guarantees that the accept() method of our listener runs on the UI thread, allowing seamless interaction with B4A's ecosystem.5.3. The processBA.raiseEventFromUI MechanismThe bridge between the Inline Java and the B4A code is the BA object (Basic4Android Context). The method raiseEventFromUI is specifically designed for this asynchronous callback scenario.Signature: raiseEventFromUI(Object sender, String eventName, Object... params)Usage: When FoldingFeature detects a change (e.g., state becomes HALF_OPENED), the Java code calls processBA.raiseEventFromUI(this, "foldstatechanged", "HALF_OPENED", "HORIZONTAL").Effect: This places the event in the main message queue. B4A picks it up and executes the Sub FoldStateChanged(State As String, Orientation As String) defined in the Basic code.6. Basic4Android Implementation SpecificsThis section details the specific coding constructs required for the B4A environment, focusing on the #AdditionalJar dependencies and the Inline Java structure.6.1. Dependency DeclarationThe user must instruct the B4A compiler to link against the external AARs (Android Archives). This is done via #AdditionalJar.
Based on the research , the following dependencies are strictly required:Basic#AdditionalJar: androidx.window:window
#AdditionalJar: androidx.window:window-java
#AdditionalJar: androidx.core:core
#AdditionalJar: kotlinx-coroutines-core
#AdditionalJar: kotlinx-coroutines-android
Note: Even though we are writing Java, the underlying library depends on Kotlin coroutines, so those JARs must be present in the classpath for the linker to succeed, even if we don't write Kotlin code directly.6.2. The Inline Java Class StructureThe code must be encapsulated in a class to manage state. A simple static method is insufficient because we need to hold a reference to the WindowInfoTracker to unregister it later.Proposed Class: FoldableListenerFields:private WindowInfoTrackerCallbackAdapter adapter;private Activity activity;private Consumer<WindowLayoutInfo> layoutConsumer;private BA processBA;private String eventName;Constructor: Initializes the fields and creates the MainThreadExecutor.Method start(): Calls adapter.addWindowLayoutInfoListener(activity, executor, layoutConsumer). This should be called from Activity_Resume.Method stop(): Calls adapter.removeWindowLayoutInfoListener(layoutConsumer). This should be called from Activity_Pause.6.3. Parsing the Feature ListThe accept method receives WindowLayoutInfo. The parsing logic must be robust:Java// Conceptual Logic
List<DisplayFeature> features = newLayoutInfo.getDisplayFeatures();
String state = "FLAT";
String orientation = "NONE";

for (DisplayFeature feature : features) {
    if (feature instanceof FoldingFeature) {
        FoldingFeature fold = (FoldingFeature) feature;
        // Map Enum to String
        if (fold.getState() == FoldingFeature.State.HALF_OPENED) state = "HALF_OPENED";
        if (fold.getOrientation() == FoldingFeature.Orientation.VERTICAL) orientation = "VERTICAL";
        else orientation = "HORIZONTAL";
    }
}
// Raise Event
processBA.raiseEventFromUI(this, eventName + "_statechanged", state, orientation);
This logic normalizes the complex object graph into simple Strings that B4A can easily consume.7. Samsung OneUI and Edge CasesThe generic API implementation described above must be stress-tested against the specific behaviors of Samsung's OneUI.7.1. Flex Mode vs. Generic Half-OpenSamsung's "Flex Mode" is the system-level response to FoldingFeature.State.HALF_OPENED. When this state is detected, OneUI may attempt to shift the application window.The "Resizeable" Requirement: For the WindowManager API to function correctly on a Z Fold, the app must be declared as resizeable in the manifest.
SetActivityAttribute(Main, android:resizeableActivity, "true")
If this is set to false, the app will run in "Compatibility Mode" (black bars), and the WindowManager might never report a fold feature because the window itself is not interacting with the fold—it's just a static rectangle floating in the middle of the screen.7.2. Multi-Window ComplexityOn a Z Fold 5, a user can run three apps simultaneously.Scenario: B4A app is in the top-right quadrant.Physical State: Device is folded 90° (Tabletop).API Report: The WindowManager will report NO folding features for the B4A app.Why? The fold runs horizontally across the screen, but the app window (top-right) might be entirely above the fold. Therefore, the fold does not intersect the window bounds.Correct Interpretation: The app should behave as if on a flat screen. The B4A logic must handle an empty getDisplayFeatures() list as a valid state (Flat), not an error.7.3. App ContinuityWhen the user unfolds the device, the Activity is usually recreated. The WindowInfoTracker must be re-initialized immediately in Activity_Create or Activity_Resume. If the developer relies on FirstTime logic in B4A (If FirstTime Then...), the listener might fail to re-attach after the fold-unfold transition. The listener registration must be tied to the current Activity instance, which changes on fold.8. Conclusion and Strategic RoadmapThe transition from standard smartphone development to foldable development requires a fundamental shift in how we perceive the display. For B4A developers, this is compounded by the shift from Java to Kotlin in the underlying Android SDK.The web-based heuristics (inline JavaScript) that served developers in the past for simple resizing are demonstrably inadequate for posture detection due to the decoupling of visual and layout viewports and the lack of hardware sensor access in standard WebViews.The exhaustive analysis confirms that the only professional-grade solution is the integration of the Jetpack WindowManager library using the Java Callback Adapter. This approach:Bypasses Kotlin Flows: Uses standard Java Consumers.Ensures Thread Safety: Uses a custom MainThreadExecutor.Provides Semantic Accuracy: Distinguishes between "Tabletop" and "Book" modes.Aligns with OEM Standards: Works on Samsung, Pixel, and Surface devices uniformly.By implementing the FoldableListener class detailed in the subsequent prompt, B4A developers can reliably detect "Flex Mode" and adapt their UI logic (e.g., splitting layouts) to provide the continuity that users expect from premium foldable hardware.9. Prompt for B4A Coding AgentContext:You are an expert Basic4Android (B4A) developer specializing in Android Java interoperability (JNI/Inline Java). The user is developing an app for Samsung Foldable devices (Z Fold/Flip) and needs to programmatically detect the "Fold State" (e.g., Flat vs. Flex Mode/Half-Open). The user has attempted to use Inline JavaScript in a WebView but found it unreliable. You must provide a native Java solution using the Jetpack WindowManager library, specifically adapted for B4A's limited Java 8 environment.Task:Generate a complete, copy-paste ready B4A code module that implements the androidx.window library to detect fold events.Technical Requirements:Dependencies (Maven Coordinates):You MUST specify the exact #AdditionalJar directives required. Crucially, you must include:androidx.window:window:1.1.0 (or newer)androidx.window:window-java:1.1.0 (MANDATORY: This contains the WindowInfoTrackerCallbackAdapter needed to avoid Kotlin syntax).androidx.core:core (To support the Consumer interface).kotlinx-coroutines-core (Required runtime dependency).Inline Java Class (FoldableListener):Write a Java class inside an #If JAVA block that encapsulates the complexity.Imports: Include androidx.window.layout.*, androidx.window.java.layout.*, java.util.concurrent.*, android.os.*.Executor: You MUST implement a custom MainThreadExecutor class inside the Java block. Use new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper()) to implement the execute method. Reason: Passing a background executor will crash B4A when raising events.Listener Registration: Use WindowInfoTrackerCallbackAdapter to register a listener.Method: addWindowLayoutInfoListener(Activity activity, Executor executor, Consumer<WindowLayoutInfo> consumer).Consumer Implementation: Implement androidx.core.util.Consumer<WindowLayoutInfo>.Note: If targeting Android 13+ APIs explicitly, check if java.util.function.Consumer is needed, but default to androidx.core.util.Consumer for maximum B4A compatibility.Fold Logic:Inside the accept(WindowLayoutInfo info) method:Iterate through info.getDisplayFeatures().Check if (feature instanceof FoldingFeature).Extract State: FLAT or HALF_OPENED.Extract Orientation: HORIZONTAL or VERTICAL.Logic Mapping:HALF_OPENED + HORIZONTAL = "TABLETOP"HALF_OPENED + VERTICAL = "BOOK"FLAT (or empty list) = "FLAT"Event Dispatch: Use processBA.raiseEventFromUI(this, "foldstatechanged", stateString, orientationString, isSeparatingBoolean) to send data back to B4A.Lifecycle Management:Provide a Start(Activity activity) method (to be called in Activity_Resume).Provide a Stop() method (to be called in Activity_Pause). This is critical to prevent memory leaks.B4A Integration Code:Show the B4A Sub Globals declaration: Private FoldDetector As JavaObject.Show the Activity_Create initialization.Show the B4A event handler: Sub FoldStateChanged(State As String, Orientation As String, IsSeparating As Boolean).Output Style:Use standard B4A syntax for the wrapper.Use pure Java 7/8 syntax for the Inline Java (No -> lambdas if possible, use anonymous inner classes new Consumer<...>() {... } to ensure maximum compatibility with older B4A compilers, or explicitly note that Java 8 desugaring must be enabled).Add comments explaining why window-java is used (to bypass Kotlin Flows).Persona:Senior Android Systems Architect. Technical, precise, and practical.Confidence Score: 5/5



